[110.383468, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\u001b7\u001b[2;77H\u001b[38;5;210m\u2584\u001b[38;5;210m\u2580\u001b[38;5;210m\u2588 \u001b[38;5;216m\u2588\u001b[38;5;216m\u2580\u001b[38;5;216m\u2584\u001b[38;5;216m\u2580\u001b[38;5;216m\u2588 \u001b[38;5;216m\u2580\u001b[38;5;222m\u2588\u001b[38;5;222m\u2580\u001b[0m\u001b[3;77H\u001b[38;5;210m\u2588\u001b[38;5;210m\u2580\u001b[38;5;210m\u2588 \u001b[38;5;216m\u2588 \u001b[38;5;216m\u2580 \u001b[38;5;216m\u2588 \u001b[38;5;222m\u2584\u001b[38;5;222m\u2588\u001b[38;5;222m\u2584\u001b[0m\u001b[4;77H\u001b]8;;https://www.superinference.org\u0007\u001b[2mwww.superinference.org\u001b[22m\u001b]8;;\u0007\u001b[0m\u001b8\u001b[1;1H\u001b[Jtiny later ones add fine detail.\r\r\n\r\r\nThat shrinking-jitter recursion is what produces statistical self-similarity (the fractal quality).\r\r\nThe power-of-two grid keeps every midpoint on an integer index, then I resample to the exact\r\r\nterminal width at the end.\r\r\n\r\r\n\u2605 Insight \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\r\r\n- **Midpoint displacement \u2248 1D diamond-square**: the `roughness` factor is effectively a fractal\r\r\n(Hurst) exponent \u2014 values near 1.0 give jagged alpine ridges, near 0.4 give smooth rolling hills.\r\r\n- **Painter's algorithm gives depth cheaply**: drawing far ridges first and near ridges over them,\r\r\nwith lighter glyphs for distant layers, simulates atmospheric perspective with no z-buffer.\r\r\n- **Reflection is just a vertical mirror + noise**: mapping row `horizon-(y-lake_top)-1` and\r\r\nrandomly dropping/rippling pixels reads convincingly as water without any physics.\r\r\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\